    "content": "Teeth are recognized as some of the most durable components of the human body, capable of persisting for extended periods even after an individual's death. This resilience makes them particularly valuable in archaeological research and forensic science, where they help identify remains and shed light on historical populations.\n\nThe hardest substance in the human body is tooth enamel, the outer layer of the tooth. This material is composed of approximately 96 percent minerals, primarily calcium and phosphorus, which contribute to its exceptional strength and resistance to wear.\n\nResearchers note that enamel can withstand years of chewing, pressure, temperature changes, and exposure to dietary acids and bacteria. This durability is why teeth are often well-preserved in ancient skeletal remains and can offer clues about diet, health, and lifestyle of past communities.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, understanding the lasting nature of teeth has practical implications. In regions where forensic infrastructure may be limited, dental records and tooth analysis can serve as reliable tools in legal investigations and disaster victim identification. Additionally, preservation of teeth in historical burial sites across Somalia and the Horn of Africa can aid in reconstructing the region's rich cultural heritage.\n\nThe mineral composition of enamel not only provides physical toughness but also makes teeth resistant to decay after death, enabling scientists to study them long after soft tissues have decomposed. This characteristic underscores the value of dental research in both modern medicine and historical study.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/24/ilkaha-oo-ka-mid-ah-qaybaha-ugu-adag-ee-jidhka-biniaadamka/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=ilkaha-oo-ka-mid-ah-qaybaha-ugu-adag-ee-jidhka-biniaadamka",
