    "content": "Mogadishu — Somalia's Federal Government has formally accelerated its national strategy for skills development and job creation, convening the inaugural meeting of the National Committee for Skills Development and Employment. The committee, comprising representatives from 13 federal ministries, met to coordinate efforts in equipping Somali workers with market-relevant skills. The initiative marks a policy shift toward tackling one of the country's most persistent socioeconomic challenges: high unemployment, particularly among the youth.\n\nThe formation of this committee reflects a growing recognition that Somalia's education and training systems often do not align with labor market demands. According to the World Bank, Somalia's youth unemployment rate is among the highest globally, with many young people lacking access to vocational training. The committee aims to bridge this gap by fostering collaboration across ministries, from education to labor, to ensure a cohesive national approach.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant. Many diaspora members possess professional expertise and entrepreneurial experience that could contribute to skills transfer. The committee's plan may open new avenues for diaspora engagement in training programs, mentorship, and investment in vocational institutions. Additionally, improved employment prospects for Somali youth could reduce the pressure on diaspora family members to remit funds, which currently constitute a substantial portion of household income.\n\nThe committee's first meeting focused on identifying priority sectors, such as construction, agriculture, and information technology, where skills shortages are acute. It also discussed partnerships with private sector stakeholders and international organizations. While details of a concrete implementation plan have not been released, the federal government has signaled its commitment to moving from policy to action.\n\nObservers note that the success of this initiative will depend on sustained political will, effective resource allocation, and overcoming obstacles such as insecurity and bureaucratic inefficiencies. The diaspora community, with its capacity for innovation and investment, can play a pivotal role in supporting these efforts.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/24/somalia-accelerates-national-skills-development-to-tackle-youth-unemployment/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=somalia-accelerates-national-skills-development-to-tackle-youth-unemployment",
