    "title": "Somali security forces raid Mogadishu slum, killing five",
    "original_title": "Gunfire, bulldozers and f-16 jets: Inside Somalia&#8217;s bloody midnight raid that emptied a Mogadishu slum",
    "slug": "somali-security-forces-raid-mogadishu-slum-killing-five",
    "summary": "At least five people were killed and more than ten wounded in a forced eviction raid on the Tookiyo settlement in Mogadishu's Yaqshid district, part of a broader demolition campaign affecting the city's poorest residents.",
    "content": "In the early hours of Sunday, Somali federal security forces conducted a raid on the Tookiyo settlement in the Yaqshid district of Mogadishu, resulting in the deaths of at least five people and injuries to more than ten others, according to local media reports. The operation, which began just after midnight on Saturday and continued into Sunday, involved the use of gunfire and heavy equipment, as well as the presence of F-16 jets overhead. The settlement, home to a number of the capital's most vulnerable residents, was forcibly cleared as part of an ongoing campaign to demolish informal settlements across the city.\n\nThe raid is one of the most violent episodes in a series of demolitions that have been underway in Mogadishu, displacing thousands of residents who had sought shelter in makeshift homes. Authorities have not publicly stated the precise reasons for the eviction, but such operations are often justified on grounds of urban development or security. However, the timing of the raid—conducted under the cover of darkness—and the heavy-handed tactics have drawn criticism from human rights observers.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this event underscores the persistent challenges facing those who remain in Mogadishu, where the lack of affordable housing and the threat of forced displacement remain acute. Many diaspora members send remittances to relatives who live in informal settlements like Tookiyo, and incidents like this raise questions about the safety of their loved ones and the future of urban policy in Somalia.\n\nThe federal government has yet to issue an official statement regarding the incident, and it remains unclear if any alternative accommodation was provided for the displaced residents. The international community has previously urged Somali authorities to conduct evictions in line with national and international standards, ensuring adequate notice and access to legal recourse.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-mogadishu-slum-eviction-raid-f16-jets/",
